ral locations where this kind of  training can happen  and also we heard about the need to  actually go and translate  those early efforts and training into  translational research where data from  data scientists or maybe students or  citizen scientists can be analyzed  locally so  indian  challenges are maybe different from  african challenges different from  challenges in the us right and so  building out that community and pool of  talent locally is critical  for those communities and so we heard  quite a few successful programs that are  being developed in those regions  and we hope that our regional teams in  africa and in india and south america  could be  part of that effort as well  now we also  heard about the india partnership  program quite a number of universities  that have signed up to that which means  that they will contribute their content  and benefit from the uh type of  resources that we make available  and that includes some of these great  universities like amit university and  others um there  we also heard today  uh and yesterday from great speakers  from both louisiana state university or  louisiana biomedical research network  from international locations uh like dr  joseph borg  uh interesting applications of uh new  technologies like single cell um from  sweden um and we heard about the t  biometric platform from the tauber  bioinformatics research center so it  really was exciting to see how these  different locations are offering  interesting insights into different  applications of bioinformatics  and we heard today  and we saw just kind of give evaluation  to student projects which are starting  to follow the lead of these experts  right so we saw projects in space in  oncology uh different applications to  different kinds of biomedical areas of  biomedical and basic research and i  think that was really exciting to see  that we actually see this translation of  ideas from you know such presentation as  this  um where an expert speak