ities for  students that master data science and  bioinformatics to um be innovative and  provide practical solutions to real  challenging problems so for example in  our last symposium we heard from  individuals that work in bioinformatics  at the therapeutic companies like cam 4  or companies that are developing  infrastructure for bioinformatics and  for teaching bioinformatics like google  or we  we had hosted panel discussions and and  experts from the faba academy that talk  about industry perspectives on  bioinformatics and biotechnology in  general so i think that's another very  important component to what we're doing  because ultimately there's not a place  in academia for everyone to succeed in  the long term and i think that knowing  about those opportunities outside and  also for companies to know that talent  is being developed and is growing um  that is also an important agenda for  what we do  uh we uh  we will talk a little bit more uh in our  next slide about the significance of uh  taking  uh bioinformatics uh  training further to address these two  important needs and and that is the  diverse types of data that continue and  develop  we heard today about nanopore we heard  yesterday about 10x genomics  probably recently most of us have been  paying attention to ultima genomics and  the price of sequencing going  dramatically down  that  leads into the need for i think a  broader framework for analysis  integration modeling and visualization  of different types of data and so that  is a recurring topic and challenge that  i think we will see being addressed in  the future and for us what that means is  that we really need to focus on  making that continuous workflow from raw  data to interpretable biological  insights that can integrate effectively  across these different  technologies and so i was really happy  to see  uh you know dr julia panoff and uh also  uh elizabeth taranenko who uh  showed advances on the t binding for  platform that we al